{
  "note": [
    "Songwriting credits for the 255 songs on the twelve studio albums, as data/songs.json",
    "groups and spells them. `writers` is the full credited songwriter line in credit order,",
    "Taylor first. She is a credited writer on all 255, so a one-name list means she wrote it",
    "alone and the list's length is the co-writer count — there is no separate solo flag, and",
    "nothing here is reduced to a yes/no.",
    "",
    "WRITER ONLY. This says nothing about who produced or who sang. A featured artist is not a",
    "co-writer unless the credit sheet says so (Sabrina Carpenter is not a writer on The Life of",
    "a Showgirl, Phoebe Bridgers not on Nothing New, Chris Stapleton not on I Bet You Think",
    "About Me), and an interpolation adds writers who never touched the record (Right Said",
    "Fred's three on Look What You Made Me Do, George Michael on Father Figure). Production is",
    "a different axis and lives in data/producers.json — a song can be Aaron Dessner's",
    "production and not his co-write.",
    "",
    "`aka` carries the person behind a credited pseudonym. The credited name is what the",
    "sleeve prints and what `writers` holds; William Bowery is the entry that matters, being",
    "how Joe Alwyn is credited across folklore and evermore.",
    "",
    "Alternate versions keep the underlying composition's credit, which is why Forever & Always",
    "(Piano Version) and State Of Grace (Acoustic Version) stay Swift-alone. A remix only gains",
    "writers where genuinely new writing was cut into it: Kendrick Lamar on Bad Blood (Remix),",
    "Ice Spice and RiotUSA on Karma (Remix). Snow On The Beach (Remix) keeps its trio.",
    "",
    "`unconfirmed` marks the one entry without an authoritative commercial credit sheet: Need,",
    "a Lover-session song whose attribution is widely circulated rather than documented. Treat",
    "it as the row to drop first, not as evidence.",
    "",
    "The twelve groups fold in EP songs, vault tracks, standalone singles, remixes and one",
    "unreleased composition (I'd Lie), because songs.json's era grouping does. This file is not",
    "a claim about original track listings.",
    "",
    "Album and track order mirror data/songs.json exactly, so the two files diff against each",
    "other. Every title here is spelled as songs.json spells it and matches it on a straight",
    "lookup — no normalising needed at the call site."
  ],
